Calc: the optimal row height pass determines the script type of a text once per string and number format.

After loading, the optimal row height pass searches each range of equal cell formats for cells to measure as edit
text (ScColumn::HasEditCells), which determines the script type (Latin, Asian, complex) of every text cell by
formatting it and running the script detection over the whole text. A large workbook repeats the same strings a
lot: on a sheet of 5.3 million text cells over 1.2 million strings this took about 9% of the loading time. For a
text cell without a conditional format the result depends only on the pooled string and the number format, so one
search keeps the results per (string, number format), holding a reference to each string, at most 131072 at a
time. A formula cell or a cell with a conditional format is determined on its own as before. The environment
variable HST_SC_SCRIPT_TYPE_MEMO_OFF (read once per process) determines every cell on its own.

New logic in sc/inc/hstscripttypememo.hxx (HST). Hooks: column.hxx (two optional parameters), column.cxx (the
memo in FindEditCellsHandler), column2.cxx (pass it on), column3.cxx (look up and store in UpdateScriptType).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new header is HST-proprietary.
Test: CppunitTest_sc_ucalc_condformat testScriptTypesOncePerStringAndFormat: after the row height pass every
cell's script type equals that of its own formatted text (Latin, Asian, complex texts; the same digits shown
plain and behind an Asian prefix format).
